function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    print("<div class=\"doc-detail-layout\"><main class=\"content doc-page doc-detail\"><div class=\"doc-title\"><h2>struct</h2></div><div class=\"doc-section signatures\"><h3>Capy</h3><pre>struct Name { field : type, ... }</pre></div><div class=\"doc-section content\"><p>A struct is a named, fixed layout of typed fields. Unlike a <code>dval</code>, its shape is checked at compile time — a missing or misspelled field is a compile error, not an empty value at runtime.</p><p>Declare one at the top level of a unit, then construct it by passing the fields in order:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Point { x : s32, y : s32 }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var origin := Point(0, 0)\n    print(origin.x, &quot;,&quot;, origin.y, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}</code></pre><h2>Fields are mutable, the binding is shared</h2><p>Reading and writing fields uses <code>.</code>:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">struct Counter { total : s32 }\n\nfunction bump(counter : Counter) {\n    counter.total = counter.total + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var counter := Counter(0)\n    bump(counter)\n    bump(counter)\n    print(counter.total, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}</code></pre><p>A struct passed to a function shares identity with the caller&#39;s value, so a function that writes a field changes what the caller sees. That is the opposite of a <code>dval</code> crossing a unit boundary, which is copied.</p><h2>Choosing between a struct and a dval</h2><p>Use a struct when the shape is known as you write the code and you want the compiler to check it. Use a <code>dval</code> when the shape arrives at runtime — request input, JSON, a database row — or when it has to cross a unit boundary, since only <code>dval</code> is copied across those.</p><p>A struct with a single field also acts as a constructor for that fi")
    print("eld&#39;s type, so <code>struct Meters { value : f64 }</code> lets <code>Meters</code> be built from an <code>f64</code> wherever one is accepted.</p><h2>Exporting a struct</h2><p><code>#exports</code> publishes a struct&#39;s layout so another unit can name it after <code>#import</code>:</p><pre><code class=\"language-capy\">#exports Shape\n\nstruct Shape { value : s32 }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(Shape(3).value, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}</code></pre></div><div class=\"doc-section example\"><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"example-language capy\"><h4>Capy</h4><pre class=\"example-source\">struct Point { x : s32, y : s32 }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var origin := Point(3, 4)\n    print(origin.x, &quot;,&quot;, origin.y, &quot;\\n&quot;)\n}\n</pre></div></div></main><aside class=\"doc-section see-also\"><h3>See also</h3><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/bool/\">bool</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/s8/\">s8</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/s16/\">s16</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/s32/\">s32</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/s64/\">s64</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/u8/\">u8</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/u16/\">u16</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/u32/\">u32</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/u64/\">u64</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/f32/\">f32</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/f64/\">f64</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/string/\">string</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/api/dval/\">dval</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/array/\">array</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/function/\">function value</a><a class=\"related-link\" href=\"/doc/type/module/\">module</a></aside></div>")
}
